FivePA — Final consolidated audit report
I have combined the three audits into one deduplicated fix list, grouping findings that describe the same underlying defect rather than repeating them under different endpoint names.

The consolidation uses the latest reviewed GitHub revision of Fivepa_Max, commit 47384ca3d9fdd97f27641ccb9937fcc534d51d01, and preserves the corrections from the deeper audit.
How to read the priorities
- Must fix — a confirmed security vulnerability, money-integrity defect, or serious correctness problem that should be resolved before production.
- Should fix — an important reliability, workflow, scalability or defense-in-depth problem.
- Optional — useful improvement or cleanup that is not, by itself, a confirmed production-blocking vulnerability.
The three audit reports contained overlapping findings. The list below consolidates them into unique fix groups, with the highest-risk issues first. A finding that still needs runtime or configuration verification is labelled accordingly rather than being presented as a proven exploit.
1. Must fix
Security, financial integrity, authorization and critical workflow correctness
#	Fix	Short problem
M1	Secure legacy offer acceptance and order creation	Legacy acceptance paths do not consistently authenticate the buyer, verify the persisted offer, and derive the seller and amount from trusted server-side records. Remove unsafe fallback behavior.
M2	Enforce object-level authorization on every order route	Some order responses, including the legacy PUT /orders/:id read branch, do not verify that the caller is a buyer, seller or authorized staff member. The confirmed issue on that branch is unauthorized disclosure, not arbitrary order mutation.
M3	Validate workspace participants and every associated order	WorkStream detail/list paths can include orders based on workspaceId or orderIds without rechecking that the order's buyer and seller belong to that workspace.
M4	Bind uploads to the correct order and WorkStream	Upload routes validate order and workspace identifiers independently, allowing inconsistent associations. Validate the complete relationship before accepting or serving a file.
M5	Fail closed when checking file ownership	Upload verification can suppress database errors, and some access checks backfill associations. An authorization check must not grant access or mutate associations when ownership cannot be established.
M6	Remove unsafe filename-based association recovery	Substring matching such as LIKE '%filename%' can associate a file with the wrong record. Use exact, immutable upload IDs and verified ownership.
M7	Prevent known-password administrator seeding in production	Demo seeding can be enabled by an environment variable even in production, and the seed data includes a privileged account with a known default password. Production must reject this configuration or make the seed path impossible.
M8	Fix withLock() so queued operations cannot be dropped	The identified lock implementation can return an existing queued promise without scheduling the new callback, causing persistence work to be silently skipped.
M9	Make escrow, order, wallet and ledger changes consistent	Money-changing operations and associated order/offer/event writes are split across operations. Failures can leave partial state or financial history without the authoritative escrow/order record.
M10	Remove destructive cleanup after possible funding	An order-creation failure can trigger order deletion after funding. Because escrow is linked with ON DELETE CASCADE, the escrow row can be deleted while related ledger or wallet-movement records remain. Use durable compensation/reconciliation, not destructive deletion.
M11	Fix payout cancellation and failure SQL parameters	The inspected wallet-update query in payout cancellation/failure has a placeholder/argument mismatch. Correct the SQL bindings and verify the wallet, payout and ledger changes are consistent.
M12	Enforce financial lifecycle rules inside the finance service	Escrow top-up logic checks escrow status and participants but does not independently enforce every order lifecycle/dispute restriction. Callers must not be the only protection around money movement.
M13	Make release and refund operations safely idempotent	Concurrent requests and retries need durable database-level protection. A repeated successful refund should return a consistent result instead of failing simply because the escrow is already terminal.
M14	Fix invoice/offer state ordering around escrow release	Some routes mark an invoice/offer as paid before the release operation is confirmed. The payment state must only commit as paid when the corresponding financial transition succeeds.
M15	Make proposal acceptance recoverable	Proposal acceptance can remain stuck in accepting if later workspace, order, offer or funding work fails. Implement an atomic state transition or a durable, recoverable workflow.
M16	Revalidate state after acquiring locks	Some routes read order/offer data before locking and do not consistently reload and recheck eligibility inside the critical section. This creates race windows for cancellation, acceptance and payment operations.
M17	Replace process-local locks as the only financial concurrency control	A local lock does not coordinate multiple server instances. Use database transactions, row locks, uniqueness constraints or appropriate distributed coordination for money-critical invariants.
M18	Add missing database relationship constraints	Message, upload, offer, workspace and order relationships lack some necessary foreign keys or consistency guarantees. Add appropriate constraints and safe migration/backfill logic without breaking legitimate legacy records.
M19	Add durable message idempotency	Client-message deduplication is check-then-insert without a corresponding unique constraint. Concurrent retries can create duplicates, while IDs built from timestamps and short random suffixes can collide.
M20	Scope message deduplication to the authenticated sender	A repeated client message ID is not consistently scoped to the sender. One participant could collide with another participant's ID and receive an incorrect deduplication result.
M21	Use one authoritative message-persistence path	recordMessage, store.addMessage and offer-message helpers can write the same logical message through multiple paths. Consolidate persistence and publish events only after successful commit.
M22	Do not let client-supplied events impersonate trusted system events	Client-originated message/event types can be downgraded or reclassified by sanitization. Server-generated events must be created by trusted server logic after the underlying transition commits.
M23	Enforce consistent account-status and participant checks across messaging	HTTP and WebSocket message paths do not consistently apply the same suspension, restriction, mute, membership and sanitization rules. Centralize shared authorization without unnecessarily blocking legitimate marketplace activity.
M24	Correct the inquiry branch's missing project/proposal checks	The normal order path validates purchase data, but the inquiry branch skips important project ownership and accepted-proposal checks. Validate inquiry relationships separately; do not incorrectly label all ordinary order amount validation as missing.
M25	Make workspace persistence failures visible and recoverable	Workspace code can mutate memory before persistence, suppress persistence errors, and overwrite relationship arrays/timestamps on conflict. Persist authoritative state first or use a transaction/outbox strategy with explicit recovery.
M26	Fix invalid or ambiguous proposal-to-offer linking	findLinkedProposalForOffer() uses an OR query with LIMIT 1 and no deterministic ordering. This can select the wrong linked proposal. Use a unique, explicit relationship.
M27	Validate delivery evidence before allowing delivery transitions	Delivery/invoice state can change without a sufficiently strong persisted deliverable invariant. Validate authorized submitters, evidence, order state and transition eligibility on the server.

Must-fix notes
- M1–M6 are authorization and cross-workstream isolation fixes; they should be addressed before production.
- M8–M18 are the core financial-integrity and durable-state work.
- M19–M23 cover message integrity and consistent access enforcement.
- These fixes must preserve FivePA's intended behavior: WorkStreams remain available after completion or cancellation, repeat business remains possible, and distinct Gig purchases can create distinct WorkStreams.
- PostgreSQL should remain the authoritative financial store. The Node.js/TypeScript backend does not need to be replaced.
2. Should fix
Important reliability, lifecycle, storage, API and scalability problems
#	Fix	Short problem
S1	Standardize offer numbering	Count-based offer numbers and timestamp suffixes are not reliable under concurrency. Use database-generated IDs or a sequence with a unique constraint.
S2	Persist offer fields consistently	Some offer details depend on metadata rather than stable, queryable fields. Standardize persistence and verify migrations and backward compatibility.
S3	Prevent offers from remaining actionable after terminal order states	Pending offers can remain actionable after completion or refund. Recheck the linked order and offer state at the time of each action.
S4	Unify cancellation and dispute state checks	Cancellation eligibility and escrow state are sometimes checked before locking, and not every path consistently respects dispute status. Revalidate immediately before the transition.
S5	Fix extension-request lifecycle handling	Extension requests have stale pending states, due-date validation issues and inconsistent free-extension accounting or timer handling.
S6	Make auto-completion and cancellation race-safe	Background transitions can overlap with manual acceptance, cancellation or review actions. Use conditional database updates and durable state transitions.
S7	Make background jobs safe across instances	Some job locks do not remain active for the whole processing interval. Use an appropriate transaction-scoped lock held for the operation, a session lock, or a durable job-claim mechanism.
S8	Add robust WebSocket connection lifecycle controls	Authentication and connection setup can leave temporary unauthenticated connections, and some early-return paths may miss cleanup. Ensure rejected sockets are closed and removed from tracking.
S9	Set transport-level WebSocket payload limits	A handler-level length check occurs after the WebSocket library has received the payload. Configure a transport-level maximum as well.
S10	Make WebSocket rate limiting proxy-aware and bounded	Forwarded IP headers can be untrusted unless configured through trusted proxies. Connection maps also need cleanup, sensible limits and heartbeat handling.
S11	Paginate message history	Some message-history paths load the full conversation without pagination, increasing memory and query costs as WorkStreams grow.
S12	Bound user-controlled text and request payloads	Text fields and some request bodies lack consistent length limits. Add server-side limits appropriate to each field and endpoint.
S13	Optimize broad order/workspace reads	Some routes load all users or orders and then filter in application memory. Use participant-scoped SQL queries and retrieve only necessary fields.
S14	Reduce repeated balance and relationship lookups	syncUserBalances() and workspace/order enrichment can perform avoidable repeated queries. Batch or scope these operations without weakening consistency.
S15	Standardize proposal and account-status guards	Proposal routes implement active/restricted account and status rules inconsistently. Reuse shared policy checks while preserving legitimate access to existing WorkStreams.
S16	Make API errors and partial-success responses consistent	Routes use different error shapes and can report success despite incomplete downstream work. Return explicit outcomes and durable operation identifiers.
S17	Handle asynchronous route failures correctly	Some async handlers perform database reads outside their main error-handling block. Ensure errors reach Express error middleware and do not leave unhandled rejections or partial responses.
S18	Make upload validation consistent across local and GCS storage	Local-file validation assumptions do not always apply to cloud objects. Enforce one storage-independent policy for ownership, allowed types, size, retrieval and deletion.
S19	Enforce an explicit upload type and URL policy	The declared extension allowlist is not a universal gate, and external URLs are accepted without a sufficiently explicit scheme policy. Validate upload content and URL schemes; do not fetch arbitrary URLs unless a separately secured feature requires it.
S20	Clean up cloud objects when database persistence fails	Cloud uploads can succeed before upload-record persistence fails. Add reliable compensation/reconciliation so unreferenced objects do not accumulate.
S21	Remove silent default storage destinations in production	A default bucket fallback can hide missing configuration. Production should fail closed when required storage configuration is absent.
S22	Compute and verify file hashes	The schema has a SHA-256 field, but the upload path examined does not populate it. Use hashes where needed for integrity and duplicate detection.
S23	Correct file-download behavior and response contracts	Upload errors and client expectations differ; filename, MIME type and download handling need consistent behavior across local and cloud storage.
S24	Improve dispute evidence and resolution validation	Evidence serialization/parsing and schema expectations are inconsistent. Validate dispute amounts, permissions and state transitions, and do not suppress failure to close a dispute.
S25	Make audit logging reliable	Audit events have gaps and inconsistent IDs, timestamps or role attribution. Record sensitive financial and administrative transitions in a durable, non-ambiguous format.
S26	Standardize notification and system-event timing	Notifications and system messages are not always aligned with the durable state transition. Emit them after commit, ideally through a transactional outbox.
S27	Validate client history-fetch and conversation-switch behavior	Failed history fetches and switching WorkStreams may clear or display stale client state. Confirm current hooks and keep failed loads distinct from genuinely empty histories.
S28	Fix client-side message deduplication and stream lifecycle behavior	Client comparison, reconnect, stream-key and auto-scroll logic need consistent message identity and lifecycle handling. The server must remain authoritative.
S29	Avoid fragile text-based activity classification	Classifying events by message text can miscategorize activity. Prefer explicit server-generated event types and structured payloads.
S30	Verify index coverage using actual query patterns	Some indexes already exist, including orders.workspace_id; the remaining index needs should be based on SQL patterns and query plans rather than blanket index creation.
S31	Review embedded database fallback behavior	PGlite initialization, shared connections and transaction serialization need environment-specific verification to ensure concurrent operations cannot interfere.
S32	Review fee calculation and order-fee snapshots for consistency	The fee module provides canonical calculations, but stored snapshots and live settings must be applied consistently. Validate configuration at its boundary and never trust caller-supplied financial totals.
S33	Make staff permissions explicit	Some staff helpers permit cancellation/refund behavior that may be broader than intended. Confirm role policy and enforce least privilege on sensitive operations.
S34	Review restricted-account write blocking consistently	Some routes apply status guards while others may not. Define and enforce a consistent policy for new transactions, messages and administrative actions without cutting off lawful access to existing records.
S35	Verify server-side timer and review-window invariants	Deadlines, review windows and auto-release behavior must derive from server timestamps and be rechecked during transitions, not trusted from client state.
S36	Improve workspace migration and hydration recovery	Legacy workspace identifiers and suppressed hydration failures can leave stale or incomplete associations. Make migrations idempotent, observable and safely retryable.
S37	Review all financial idempotency keys and replay responses	Not every money-changing endpoint has equally strong idempotency. Persist request identity, enforce uniqueness and reject replays whose payload differs from the original.
S38	Verify payout, refund and reconciliation behavior end to end	Besides the confirmed SQL binding bug, review wallet availability, payout state, ledger movements and retry behavior as one workflow. Runtime/database verification is still needed.
S39	Audit production secrets, tracked data and deployment configuration	Inspect tracked runtime files, secret exposure, SSL/database settings, retention and error reporting. .gitignore does not remove files already tracked.
S40	Verify the full frontend against the latest server behavior	Some earlier claims about fabricated UI data, attachment-only messages, HTTP-success handling and client token storage remain partially verified. Confirm each path before changing it.

Should-fix notes
These are not all equal in urgency. S4–S7 and S24–S26 are particularly important for preventing inconsistent order, dispute and timer states. S8–S10 are important for production resilience. S13–S14 become increasingly important as marketplace traffic and conversation history grow.

The extension and review fixes must preserve the intended product rules, including the existing Gig/Project distinction and the configured review windows. Do not add arbitrary seller limits or block legitimate repeat work as a shortcut.
3. Optional improvements
Useful polish, maintainability and operational improvements after the security and integrity work
#	Improvement	Short problem
O1	Standardize currency and branding	Some labels and UI messages use inconsistent currency or sender/brand labels. Keep all monetary values in USD and use consistent FivePA terminology.
O2	Improve activity and notification wording	Some activity messages rely on technical or text-derived labels. Make descriptions clearer while preserving structured event types underneath.
O3	Document permanent WorkStream retention	Current code appears to retain uploads/messages intentionally. Document the retention behavior and storage implications before introducing cleanup. Do not automatically delete legitimate work history.
O4	Improve non-sensitive operational diagnostics	Add useful structured logging and correlation IDs for failed workflows, without logging passwords, tokens, private message content or sensitive financial details.
O5	Clean up stale or duplicate implementations	Where multiple helpers implement the same business rule, consolidate them after confirming all callers and backward compatibility. Avoid broad refactors during urgent security fixes.
O6	Improve user-facing error messages	Where a request fails safely, provide consistent messages that explain the next action without exposing internal stack traces or sensitive implementation details.
O7	Improve maintainability of shared policy rules	Keep permissions, status transitions, upload validation and fee calculation in shared server-side policy/service boundaries rather than repeating logic across routes.
O8	Improve repository and release documentation	Document required production environment variables, database migrations, storage setup, recovery procedures and deployment safeguards.


4. Important findings deliberately not counted as separate fixes
These are either already covered above, require verification, or were overstated in an earlier report. They should not become duplicate tickets.
- Public user-profile privacy: the inspected GET /users/:id route uses sanitizePublicUser() for ordinary public access. The earlier claim that it directly returns safeUser with private fields is stale for the reviewed revision. Keep the public sanitizer and verify it remains applied to every public-profile path.
- General order-amount validation: the normal purchase path does validate amounts. The confirmed concern is the separate inquiry branch's missing project/proposal relationship checks.
- Arbitrary order mutation through PUT /orders/:id: the inspected ordinary branch exposes an order without a participant check; it was not established as a general arbitrary-update primitive.
- All database relationships missing: the schema already contains some useful foreign keys, indexes and financial constraints. Add the missing specific constraints instead of replacing the schema wholesale.
- Every background job being unprotected: some protections exist, but they are not consistently effective. The earnings-job transaction-scoped advisory lock is the specific confirmed concern.
- Automatic upload deletion: the current retention behavior appears intentional. Review storage cost and retention policy before changing it.
- Missing test runner: run-tests.js exists. Its reliability has not been verified.
- Unverified frontend claims: some client-hook and UI concerns remain partial until the complete current frontend path is checked. They are not presented here as confirmed exploits.
- Every concurrency concern being exploitable: some require concurrent requests, multiple server instances or a specific failure sequence. They still merit fixes where the underlying invariant is not enforced durably.
5. Recommended implementation order
The following order groups the fixes into manageable batches. It is a sequence of implementation priorities, not a claim that the batches have already been implemented or tested.

Batch 1 — Access control and isolation

M1–M7, M22–M24

Secure legacy routes, verify participants and workspace membership, correct file associations, fail closed on ownership checks, prevent production demo-admin seeding, and protect trusted system events.




Batch 2 — Financial integrity

M8–M18, M26

Fix locking, payout SQL, funding/release/refund consistency, dispute-aware top-ups, proposal recovery, state revalidation and database constraints.




Batch 3 — Messages and durable WorkStreams

M19–M21, M25, M27

Add durable idempotency, sender scoping, one persistence authority, reliable workspace persistence and validated delivery transitions.




Batch 4 — Lifecycle and background reliability

S1–S7, S15–S17, S24–S26, S35–S38

Resolve cancellation, dispute, extension, auto-release, proposal and payout lifecycle inconsistencies.




Batch 5 — Uploads, WebSockets and scalability

S8–S14, S18–S23, S29–S31, S36, S39–S40

Standardize storage, bound requests, improve query efficiency and confirm remaining frontend and deployment concerns.




Batch 6 — Optional polish

O1–O8

Improve labels, documentation, diagnostics and maintainability after the higher-risk defects are resolved.



Final conclusion
The three audits converge on four major risk areas: access control, financial consistency, WorkStream/file isolation, and unreliable multi-step state transitions. These are more important than cosmetic issues or broad refactoring.

The consolidated list above removes duplicate descriptions, preserves the findings that remain supported by the reviewed source, and corrects earlier overstatements. It should be used as the master fix register rather than treating all 126 original report entries as separate tasks.

One qualification remains important: this is a source-level consolidation against the reviewed GitHub commit. A live production-readiness sign-off still requires verification of deployment configuration, database behavior and the affected execution paths. I did not run the application, build, typecheck or tests during this audit.

All fixes should preserve FivePA's open marketplace model, keep Node.js/TypeScript, use PostgreSQL as the authoritative financial persistence layer, and avoid unnecessary restrictions on normal buyers and sellers. 
















